{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"body":"## Frozen literals and the anchoring fix — measured on `757ca4852d7`\n\nThe plan measured its live-reference set on `ce5795c6a5c`. Re-measured on current `origin/main` `757ca4852d7` with the word-anchored form AC1 now mandates, the set is **unchanged** — exactly 2 hits across all 22 basenames:\n\n| Hit | Classification | Action |\n|---|---|---|\n| `.github/mechanism-baseline.json:61` — `autouse_fixture::tests/unit/reconciler/state/test_backend_scalar_surface_heldout.py::_clean` | stale baseline row, named in Testing | none; the two `_clean` fixtures are byte-identical, dedupe keeps the pinned base copy, the twin's key buckets `stale`, `--check` exits 0 |\n| `tests/fixtures/dc_wiki_corpus/code_arrow.json:16` | frozen pre-fix literal — a captured ticket body in a generated wiki-render corpus, naming `test_gate_error_sidecar_heldout.py` | none; recorded here per AC1 |\n\n`scripts/criteria-vocabulary-allowlist.txt` pins none of these 22 paths, as the plan predicted. Confirmed by grep, not assumed.\n\n### The attribution rule AC1's sweep requires\n\nAC1's grep, run naively, also reports 45 hits that are **this change's own provenance records**: the merged docstring line `Held-out oracles merged from <name>_heldout.py: …` and the mandated divider `# ── held-out oracles (merged from <name>_heldout.py) ──`, each sitting in the very base module that absorbed that twin.\n\nThose are not violations, and AC1 says so in its own amendment text, which describes the identical lines S1 `361f` landed as \"the provenance comments at `tests/unit/test_attest_registry.py:14,:84`\" — cited as expected content whose only problem was a substring misattribution. The Testing section states the rule directly: \"Attribute every hit to its owning path before acting on it.\"\n\nSo the sweep is run as: for basename `X_heldout.py`, exclude hits inside `X.py`, the base module that owns that provenance. Everything else must fall in the exception set. Measured that way the sweep returns **exactly the 2 rows above and nothing else**.\n\nWithout that rule AC1 and the Approach contradict each other: the Approach *mandates* a divider naming the deleted file, so no conforming implementation could ever return zero hits.\n\n### Measurements\n\n- 22 pairs merged; **380** top-level definitions in, **380** out (195 base + 236 held − 51 identical dedupes), zero mismatches.\n- **14 renames over 7 pairs**, matching the plan's enumerated set name for name, including `test_canonical_link_diff` correctly carrying **0** renames as the plan's correction states.\n- `git status` shows exactly the 22 base modules modified and the 22 twins deleted — `ruff format` touched nothing else in those 3 directories.\n- `ruff check` clean; `python scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py --check` exits 0.\n\n### One mechanical hazard worth recording\n\nRenaming lengthened 2 lines in `test_canonical_outbound_equivalence.py` past the 100-character limit (`_jira` → `_jira_heldout` adds 8 characters per call), so `ruff check --fix` left 7 E501 errors that only `ruff format` could resolve. A merge pass that runs `--fix` alone and stops will report failures that are not defects in the merge."},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1793494116077596001,"uuid":"7331acd7-a8ce-4b88-aa2f-9978cc31220d"}